## ProGuard / R8 Security Rules

```kotlin
// app/build.gradle.kts
android {
    buildTypes {
        release {
            isMinifyEnabled = true
            isShrinkResources = true
            proguardFiles(
                getDefaultProguardFile("proguard-android-optimize.txt"),
                "proguard-rules.pro",
                "proguard-security.pro",
            )
        }
    }
}
```

```properties
# gradle.properties
android.enableR8.fullMode=true
```

```proguard
# proguard-security.pro

# 1) Strip all Log.* calls in release — prevents PII leakage via logcat.
-assumenosideeffects class android.util.Log {
    public static *** v(...);
    public static *** d(...);
    public static *** i(...);
    public static *** w(...);
    public static *** e(...);
    public static *** wtf(...);
}

# 2) Strip println/Timber.d, etc. — anything that emits to logcat in release.
-assumenosideeffects class kotlin.io.ConsoleKt {
    public static *** println(...);
}
-assumenosideeffects class timber.log.Timber {
    public static *** v(...);
    public static *** d(...);
    public static *** i(...);
}

# 3) Don't keep source file names or line numbers in stack traces sent to users.
#    Map file is still generated for Play Console upload.
-renamesourcefileattribute SourceFile
-keepattributes SourceFile,LineNumberTable

# 4) Aggressively obfuscate sensitive packages.
-repackageclasses 'a'
-allowaccessmodification

# 5) Keep classes that the runtime needs by reflection — but do not over-keep.
#    Only add specific @Keep'd classes, not whole packages.

# 6) Strip BuildConfig debug fields (DEBUG flag stays; secrets stripped if marked).
-assumevalues class **.BuildConfig {
    public static final boolean DEBUG return false;
}
```

Verification:
- `./gradlew :app:assembleRelease`
- `jadx-gui app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk` — confirm class names are obfuscated and no `Log.d("token: …")` strings remain.
- Upload the `mapping.txt` to Play Console (or your crash reporter) so production stacks remain readable internally.

